[COMMENTS]
STATION,Compound broad-crested weir gauging a rural predominantly Chalk catchment largely overlain with Boulder Clay, significant artificial influences. GENERAL: Compound broad-crested weir, 22.3m broad (in trapezoidal section) with central notch 3m broad, 0.23m deep. Previously called Chesterford Mill, and included millstream flow through old mill on the side of existing river channel. STATION HYDROMETRY: Flow remains modular to 1.06m. One peak flow rating applied across period of record. Stage record before 14 May 1969 truncated at around 0.46m; peaks are therefore unknown over this period and the peak flow record present here starts on 14 May 1969. Prior to 1964, millstream flow was added to main weir rating using a rating table. FLOW RECORD: Full period of record peak flow data reviewed and released in October 2018 (WINFAP Files v7) and August 2025 (WINFAP Files v14). FLOW REGIME: Significant gw abstractions for PWS, with additional abstractions for industry/agriculture. Runoff increased by effluent returns.
CATCHMENT,Predominantly Chalk largely overlain by Boulder Clay. Rural, predominantly arable land use.
Qmed Suitability,Although not gauged to within 30% of QMED, flow is contained and remains modular. Theoretical rating is expected to perform well.
Pooling Suitability,Although no gaugings beyond QMED, flow is contained and remains modular throughout. Theoretical rating is expected to perform well.
[END]
